<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- D-10: the app was fully offline through v1.0 and declared no permissions.
         Inert until an SDK uses it; declared explicitly so the store permission
         diff is a deliberate commit rather than an implicit manifest-merge result.
         Note that ads-mobile-sdk merges INTERNET in regardless, so this line changes
         nothing about the merged output — which is the point. The permission is now
         something this repository decided to request, visible in its history, rather
         than something that arrived with a dependency bump. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!-- UI-ICON-01. android:icon and android:roundIcon deliberately point at the SAME
         resource. @mipmap/ic_launcher is an adaptive icon on API 26+, which already
         answers every mask the launcher can ask for, including round ones; a separate
         round asset would be the same artwork stored twice and would drift. On API 24-25
         the resource resolves to the mipmap-anydpi layer-list instead, which carries its
         own rounded silhouette.

         Both attributes request no permission and declare no component, so the merged
         permission set that ManifestSurfaceTest asserts by equality is unchanged.

         android:localeConfig (docs/ux/localisation-tr.md D-9, §7.4) names the languages Android
         13+ offers for this app in its per-app language setting, the only language picker; below
         API 33 the attribute is not read and the app follows the system language. It requests no
         permission either. -->
    <application
        android:name=".App"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:localeConfig="@xml/locales_config"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.PegSolitaire">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.PegSolitaire">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!-- CONS-01. This tag is a UMP requirement, NOT a GMA one, and the distinction is
             the only reason it is not obviously redundant: the GMA Next-Gen SDK takes the
             AdMob application ID programmatically through InitializationConfig (see
             UmpConsentGateway), so nothing about MobileAds.initialize needs a manifest
             entry. The User Messaging Platform SDK does read it from here, and its absence
             produces a "missing application ID" crash on the first UMP call — which, since
             consent runs before anything else, means at cold start.

             The value is the GENERATED string resource, never a literal. app/build.gradle.kts
             emits admob_app_id per build type via resValue(), so debug carries Google's test
             app ID and release the real one with no plumbing here. An inline literal ID
             would also fail the adIdLiteralCheck gate, which scans all of src/main — this
             comment deliberately does not spell one out, for the same reason.

             This is a <meta-data> and requests no permission: the merged permission set that
             ManifestSurfaceTest asserts by equality is unchanged by it. -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.gms.ads.APPLICATION_ID"
            android:value="@string/admob_app_id" />

        <!-- PGS-01. Required by PlayGamesSdk.initialize() and every
             PlayGames.get*Client(...) lookup in platform/games/PlayGamesService.kt: the
             SDK reads the Games services project ID from here, and without the tag those
             calls fail at runtime. Phase 6 built the resource for this tag and left the
             tag itself unwritten because nothing consumed it yet — this is the phase that
             consumes it.

             The value is the GENERATED string resource, exactly as the AdMob tag above
             uses admob_app_id: app/build.gradle.kts emits game_services_project_id per
             build type via resValue(). It MUST stay a @string reference rather than an
             inline value — the project ID is numeric, and a bare numeric android:value is
             parsed as an integer rather than a string, which the SDK rejects.

             Placed immediately above the PlayGamesInitProvider removal below so the two
             Play-Games manifest entries stay adjacent: that removal comment names this
             phase as the explicit-init owner, and this tag is what explicit init needs.

             A <meta-data> requests no permission, so the merged permission set that
             ManifestSurfaceTest asserts by equality is unchanged by it. -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.gms.games.APP_ID"
            android:value="@string/game_services_project_id" />

        <!-- D-07, as corrected by D-17. play-services-games-v2 declares
             PlayGamesInitProvider (authority ${applicationId}.playgamesinitprovider,
             initOrder 99), and a ContentProvider at that init order runs before
             App.onCreate completes, firing automatic Play Games sign-in on a real
             device before this app has decided anything.

             The reason for removing it is production cold start plus the downstream
             CONS-02 requirement: initialisation must happen only for a player who
             chose it. PlayGamesSdk.initialize now runs in App.onCreate, gated on the
             persisted play_games_opted_in flag (GamesService.initializeIfOptedIn) —
             i.e. at the same process-start moment this provider would have used, but
             only for a player who opted in, which is precisely the distinction the
             provider cannot make. The removal therefore still stands: the provider
             would initialise unconditionally, for everyone, before the app has
             decided anything.

             It is NOT about test isolation — Robolectric 4.16 does not instantiate
             manifest ContentProviders at startup (verified from its bytecode:
             AndroidTestEnvironment has no provider loop, and ShadowContentResolver
             instantiates lazily on a ContentResolver call naming the authority), so
             the JVM suite was never at risk and removing this buys it nothing.

             There is deliberately NO MobileAdsInitProvider counterpart. The GMA
             Next-Gen SDK declares no ContentProvider at all and requires an explicit
             MobileAds.initialize() — verified against ads-mobile-sdk 1.4.0's AAR
             manifest, 2026-08-27. Writing a removal for it would be a harmless no-op
             that documents a belief which is false.

             android:name is what the merger keys on; the authority is carried here
             because it documents what is being removed. -->
        <provider
            android:name="com.google.android.gms.games.provider.PlayGamesInitProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.playgamesinitprovider"
            tools:node="remove" />
    </application>
</manifest>
